   - This lesson uses the price form only through that link and does not re-derive it.
   - No portfolio with zero cost at date 0 has a payoff at \(\tau\) that is nonnegative in every state and positive in some state.
   - (a state-free portfolio, meaning one whose payoff is the same in every state at \(\tau\), and the forward price)
   - Conclusions: (i) the portfolio of one long unit of the asset, a loan of \(S\) at date 0 repaid as \(S/D\), and a short forward at \(F'\) has zero cost at date 0 and pays \(F'-S/D\) at \(\tau\) in every state.
   - (ii) If \(F'>S/D\), the portfolio of (i) costs zero and pays \(F'-S/D>0\) in every state, which A4 forbids.
   - Its cost is zero, and at \(\tau\) the forward delivers the asset that covers the short sale, so the payoff is \(S/D-F'>0\) in every state, which A4 also forbids.
